#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105486 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3977d627-65bc-5f36-8c25-7d47b515f383" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3566" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=9x^4$ and $q(x)=3x^4$, what is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}$?" :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - ∞)(p(x))/(q(x)) &= lim_(x → - ∞)\\frac{9x^{4}}{3x^{4}} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → - ∞)3 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/id #uuid "88c456d9-e5d3-5b33-a847-03f201751c19" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-36" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(-12x^{12}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The graph $y=x^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^{12} = \\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-12$ causes $y=-12x^{12}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → ∞)(-12x^{12}) =-lim_(x → ∞)(x^{12}) = - ∞\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/id #uuid "551f2aa0-8589-5725-abe6-de1b7eca0327" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-37" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - ∞)\\frac{x^{3}}{2} &= lim_(x → - ∞)(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x^{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → - ∞)x^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=x^3$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} x^3 = -\\infty\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ndoes not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → - ∞)x^{3} &= lim_(x → - ∞)x^{3} \\\\\n&= - ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "969d5f07-c5ce-5601-b2de-0d23eb15809b" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-39" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow -\\infty} (44-3y^4-9y^{12}) \\,$." :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(y → - ∞)(44 - 3y^{4}- 9y^{12}) &= lim_(y → - ∞)(-9y^{12}) \\\\\n&=-lim_(y → - ∞)(y^{12}) \\\\\n&= - ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd39d08a-28d3-552c-a980-5df424f6a7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243976} {:answer/id #uuid "c15176a3-1b23-596e-b17c-02491bed22ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd39d08a-28d3-552c-a980-5df424f6a7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243976} :answer-field/id #uuid "5281085a-3c0e-5962-ac73-b1c25c1d2ae2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243975}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68755e41-b4eb-5dc8-908b-a53eae1bfe1a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-300762" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13{x}^{13})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }13{x}^{13} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13\\cdot {x}^{13}) \\\\  & =13\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{13}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $13$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13{x}^{13})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "852eda5a-fccf-5472-a04a-a7347400b59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "852eda5a-fccf-5472-a04a-a7347400b59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243980} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1cfda2-7434-581d-b5b1-6dd349750668" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243979}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a871e7e7-8cf4-59fa-8019-b14e04249778" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-300766" :question/problem "If $p(x)=10{x}^{8}$ and $q(x)=2{x}^{8},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10{x}^{8})}{(2{x}^{8})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b189bc-e353-56e7-b35e-490e5768f4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b189bc-e353-56e7-b35e-490e5768f4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243983} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a47ab1-576b-547c-8657-c38701392840" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243982}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ffbc29c-ac41-5b4d-9058-2b01467fe05a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-301603" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930904/assets/2fd351e75519ab242ba99028214cbdd35c61cea6acdf69a39eb30395e2f7eaaa.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5})=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the root rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[5]{f(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-32.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{-32} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-2$ causes $y=-2{x}^{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{6})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3376395-9a94-5b75-9047-79428f921012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3376395-9a94-5b75-9047-79428f921012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243986} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5b72fd8-9b2e-5568-b3c9-50fa449c0230" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243985}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "501c8fcc-e277-5bee-85b9-ccc180b14c2c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-301609" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First note that $ln⁡(0.9)<0.$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{4}$ looks like an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $ln⁡(0.9)$ causes $y=ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{4})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e445182a-9d22-50fa-884c-cc6da48707f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e445182a-9d22-50fa-884c-cc6da48707f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243989} :answer-field/id #uuid "f613d2cb-38d1-5f1e-ac6e-57a420181f19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243988}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c99095c-ef3c-5150-9fbc-24c606d52761" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-301610" :question/problem "If $p(x)=-20{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-20{x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }((-20\\cdot \\frac{1}{5})\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-4$ causes $y=-4{x}^{2}$ to be a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "feeb959e-4ffb-5c20-badf-48dd9b4cb091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243992} {:answer/id #uuid "195984d0-e524-5533-81eb-04e9a6d348c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "195984d0-e524-5533-81eb-04e9a6d348c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243993} :answer-field/id #uuid "75bc54b5-f1d1-560b-859d-a9234d95610d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243991}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "195543a3-3781-5c30-8836-03060a38637b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-302056" :question/problem "If $p(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=9{x}^{6},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{4}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{4}\\cdot (-5{x}^{2})=-5{x}^{6}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}}{(9{x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{5}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3431c9ad-3de8-5f6b-98b3-c0f145772b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3431c9ad-3de8-5f6b-98b3-c0f145772b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243996} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6ee83b-edad-50ad-a28f-54f54db808ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243995}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f83014e-2628-5f35-ab54-6db1659f5989" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-336891" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4\\cdot {x}^{13}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{13}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $4$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13})=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b685083-a288-5948-8f2b-fdc648a7b75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b685083-a288-5948-8f2b-fdc648a7b75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243999} :answer-field/id #uuid "76908e74-15e1-5188-b779-5628ee8ca367" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243998}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5cab8eb-177c-5cf6-8c20-f7ff9d55c831" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-336894" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6\\cdot {x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{7}$ is a power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-6$ changes the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}=\\infty . & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1724db9-d4b2-59db-aac3-4cddfd62ba40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244002} {:answer/id #uuid "39ca86e5-21a5-51db-ac71-65ca83009229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1724db9-d4b2-59db-aac3-4cddfd62ba40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244002} :answer-field/id #uuid "99470452-52c4-546b-8336-1c8238c94ee5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244001}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "194e3798-ad9a-5699-a2c1-0181705e904b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-336948" :question/problem "If $f(x)=9{x}^{6}$ and $g(x)=-4{x}^{6},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=$  \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{(-4{x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "526672e8-707f-562d-8d0a-cf39142b627e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "526672e8-707f-562d-8d0a-cf39142b627e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244006} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d47786-5d89-5640-8e12-eb53d52264b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "415eb59a-b4e8-56cf-bdb1-7537f27912f0" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-336978" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}-7{x}^{3}+3x-5)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}-7{x}^{3}+3x-5) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2116b91d-942d-55f9-991e-2e0f0b3137e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244009} {:answer/id #uuid "d9efb21c-85a6-5cb1-b6c6-bace06b8532f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2116b91d-942d-55f9-991e-2e0f0b3137e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244009} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a22bae-037a-505d-a992-313946cfc7e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3101e52c-4716-50c3-9e67-5ebdf48e198e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-336995" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}+2x-9)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}+2x-9) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c54a808a-8621-5260-bd0b-58c6679e6db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c54a808a-8621-5260-bd0b-58c6679e6db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244013} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5ea9956-92dd-5705-a74b-9ddd735f4098" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244012}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6411d54-adf5-5ec6-960c-d076b496ad50" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-337003" :question/problem "${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(5{t}^{3}+2)}^{4}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(5{t}^{3}+2{)}^{4} & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(5{t}^{3}{)}^{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }625{t}^{12} \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deb0c971-e4a9-5c97-9573-44b571dda1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deb0c971-e4a9-5c97-9573-44b571dda1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244016} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c9e435-c7bd-5177-9513-4fb84ce5e053" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244015}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5560974b-0cf1-5315-9c40-bae74e0b7a14" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-337032" :question/problem "${lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }{(4{t}^{3}-t)}^{4}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }{(4{t}^{3}-t)}^{4} & ={lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }{(4{t}^{3})}^{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }256{t}^{12} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to -\\infty }{t}^{12} \\\\  & =256\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "844e69f5-bf0e-5220-a337-129f2e7b58c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103746} {:answer/id #uuid "86e72c35-b802-51d7-9b81-24285d48f52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103747} {:answer/id #uuid "c8385ccd-d194-5907-87a1-4f1d2235b281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103748} {:answer/id #uuid "cafe89ee-461c-5362-875a-1eca2b94a01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103749} {:answer/id #uuid "f4119f9f-bb91-5151-b690-05e3804a4893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103750} {:answer/id #uuid "ab633047-54e9-510b-a7ea-63f8cec1a71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8385ccd-d194-5907-87a1-4f1d2235b281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103748} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade21269-3eba-5437-a026-e6edf7ec7052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103745}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c7529a5-2ffe-53f0-9bca-cd9b5d97a802" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-35192" :question/problem "Evaluate $lim_(x → - ∞)(-\\frac{x^{5}}{4})$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db65adba-677f-5a39-a680-a0bbeee58248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244020} {:answer/id #uuid "d09e9636-12e1-517e-9f94-6e18927ef338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244021} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcd824b-1f5f-5382-857f-108a99734b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244022} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc870a7-0317-549a-bc8b-ffb308517873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244023} {:answer/id #uuid "24ce91b1-3018-5243-89b1-bf753cea9e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dcd824b-1f5f-5382-857f-108a99734b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244022} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea3eb246-d689-590c-87ee-f58d559957f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244019}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba9e0b5e-ccfa-5ee4-8925-c9bb35be0148" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-35194" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }5{x}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{3}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{3})=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03120a11-37f0-519b-84a3-ebf01005aff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244027} {:answer/id #uuid "63eb7aca-0d25-54e9-9815-8e7de92cc624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244028} {:answer/id #uuid "3f34a6f5-2d43-5a3e-880a-469aeb9abb1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244029} {:answer/id #uuid "1e41fd19-d0da-5a59-af5e-53883bf0853c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244030} {:answer/id #uuid "988c9df7-7d31-5c0b-84c8-6101237506c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63eb7aca-0d25-54e9-9815-8e7de92cc624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244028} :answer-field/id #uuid "6723670a-201b-5223-93b1-babf84c63637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddcba296-0d72-577a-b13c-e8483070e583" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-35243" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930904/assets/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd7de165-3d86-5dff-ab1e-cc78f1586263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244034} {:answer/id #uuid "d13384c7-1849-591d-81fe-19dd2a4ee35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244035} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7e0bdd-7a00-56f1-9a2b-a089f6557c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244036} {:answer/id #uuid "c727946b-612c-5efb-acae-e80b2ca3b404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244037} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dca826-5f3f-5f40-aa59-c28754a89bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13384c7-1849-591d-81fe-19dd2a4ee35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244035} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c39d863-1f5d-58ed-b3c0-63e893081924" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244033}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da57a6bc-d0db-59f4-81d7-c54fef1172ef" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-35379" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\,(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f698ed-7c90-5195-9f14-9104f4bc1bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244041} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1e97ce-25e9-5fae-8b23-258e39ef8fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244042} {:answer/id #uuid "daf3d195-184c-58a6-afe2-5f55c4e98187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244043} {:answer/id #uuid "294743e3-2d58-57b6-b4d5-ccb6cfaa55fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244044} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd8ba0b-01a6-514c-b193-699d7c3d3925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daf3d195-184c-58a6-afe2-5f55c4e98187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244043} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f67757-df2c-509f-adc7-64f868099c31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244040}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89aeb88c-5cce-526e-a20e-88a320331ce3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-67575" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $q(x)=3{x}^{3}$, what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3270d57-cb62-58c0-a7c5-e2f62cb86f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244048} {:answer/id #uuid "511608dc-14a7-580c-bf04-5a6beb6a21a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244049} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d4bc8d-7cd1-5045-8274-3f2fbb0b38dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244050} {:answer/id #uuid "f49c1039-5442-5fe2-ae3d-594318f589fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244051} {:answer/id #uuid "8d57f482-9914-5ecf-be66-8fe5cb9f61fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d4bc8d-7cd1-5045-8274-3f2fbb0b38dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244050} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b9c9714-771c-5836-8b4d-459d9b82279e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244047}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aae45131-7b91-548f-a9c2-0c60436818d2" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-78074" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)={x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=-7{x}^{4},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(-7{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3bce1b5-31fa-59c4-bac5-47aa24d4c77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244055} {:answer/id #uuid "76ad6d1c-8f7d-5cbe-b53e-9841bd998245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244056} {:answer/id #uuid "f6982edd-6b28-5c0f-b43c-18cba4b166ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244057} {:answer/id #uuid "7aaada08-2f17-57ff-81fe-42e776277441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244058} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c857c6-a4c2-55c5-94ee-96b3afb71185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76ad6d1c-8f7d-5cbe-b53e-9841bd998245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244056} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea2eabd-e63c-548e-8aa5-030211c92ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04e03d8c-083d-55f6-af8d-a9a0454d8da3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-99275" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12}).$" :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{12}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $8$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12})={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
